--TEST--
A keyed `foreach` over a `Core\ObjectMap` walks the map as it was when the loop began: an added entry is not visited, a removed one is still visited, and a `set` on a later entry does not change the value the loop gives — `rule:iteration/keyed-interfaces`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:iteration/keys-and-values-from-a-map
final class Tag {
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(string $name) {
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

Tag $red = new Tag("red");
Tag $blue = new Tag("blue");
Tag $green = new Tag("green");

Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int> $weights = new Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int>();
$weights->set($red, 3);
$weights->set($blue, 7);
$weights->set($green, 9);

// In the first step, the body adds `gold`, removes `blue` and sets `green` to 100.
// The loop still prints `red=3`, `blue=7` and `green=9`, and it does not print `gold`.
foreach ($weights as Tag $tag => int $n) {
    echo $tag->name, "=", $n, "\n";
    if ($tag->name == "red") {
        $weights->set(new Tag("gold"), 1);
        $weights->remove($blue);
        $weights->set($green, 100);
    }
}

// After the loop, the map has the changes the body made.
echo $weights->count(), "\n";
foreach ($weights as Tag $after => int $value) {
    echo $after->name, "=", $value, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
red=3
blue=7
green=9
3
red=3
green=100
gold=1
